<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a deletion formula for the inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial and the inverse <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial of a matroid. Our formulas provide analogues to the deletion formulas of Braden–Vysogorets for Kazhdan–Lusztig and <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials. We discuss several consequences, which include closed formulas and recursions for these invariants on uniform matroids, projective geometries, glued cycles, and arbitrary matroids of corank <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. As a relevant application of our deletion formula, we show the existence of a matroid of rank <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math> which disproves a conjecture of Xie and Zhang concerning a real-rootedness property of inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary: 05B35
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a fundamental invariant associated with any matroid <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. It was introduced by Elias, Proudfoot, and Wakefield in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and exhibits formal similarities to the Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials defined for Bruhat intervals of Coxeter groups. The coefficients of <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> depend only on the lattice of flats <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M})</annotation></semantics></math> of the matroid and, in fact, they can be expressed as signed integer combinations of the flag Whitney numbers counting chains of flats with specified ranks (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> plays a significant role in the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">singular</em> Hodge theory of matroids developed by Braden, Huh, Matherne, Proudfoot, and Wang in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Two further polynomial invariants that are often studied in this context are the <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of a matroid</em>, introduced by Gao and Xie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and denoted <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial introduced by Proudfoot, Xu, and Young <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and denoted <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. One of the main contributions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> consists of interpreting these three polynomials in the following way:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hilbert series of the stalk of the intersection cohomology module of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> at the empty set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 1.9]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hilbert series of the intersection cohomology module of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 1.9]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The coefficient of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the multiplicity of the trivial module in the degree <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rk(\mathsf{M})-2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rk(\mathsf{M})-2k</annotation></semantics></math> piece of the Rouquier complex of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Proposition 8.21]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above interpretations imply that these three polynomials have non-negative coefficients for every matroid <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, computing the above polynomials is often a daunting task even for small matroids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Very recently, Gao, Ruan, and Xie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> studied a fourth polynomial, called the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inverse <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial</em>, denoted by <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. While the nonnegativity of the coefficients of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the nonnegativity for <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, at the moment we lack an interpretation for the coefficients of <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as graded dimensions (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Problem 1]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, Braden and Vysogorets presented a formula that relates the Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial
of a matroid <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to that of the matroid <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}\smallsetminus i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}\smallsetminus i</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by deleting an element <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as various restrictions and contractions of <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for a matroid <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is not a coloop, their main result is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 2.8]</cite>)</h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a loopless matroid of rank <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in E</annotation></semantics></math> be an element of the ground set that is not a coloop. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P_{\mathsf{M}\smallsetminus\{i\}}(x)-xP_{\mathsf{M}/\{i\}}(x)+\sum_{F\in{S}_{i}}\uptau\left(\mathsf{M}/{(F\cup\{i\})}\right)\,x^{\frac{k-\rk(F)}{2}}\,P_{\mathsf{M}|_{F}}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P_{\mathsf{M}\smallsetminus\{i\}}(x)-xP_{\mathsf{M}/\{i\}}(x)+\sum_{F\in{S}_{i}}\uptau\left(\mathsf{M}/{(F\cup\{i\})}\right)\,x^{\frac{k-\rk(F)}{2}}\,P_{\mathsf{M}|_{F}}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
