<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Glued cycles</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us denote by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the graphic matroid associated to a cycle of length <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{n}=\mathsf{U}_{n-1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{n}=\mathsf{U}_{n-1,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graphic matroid that results from gluing two cycles of length <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> along a common edge (see Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2. Glued cycles ‣ 4. Some special cases and examples ‣ Inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomialsof matroids under deletion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an example). Deleting this common edge results in the graphic matroid <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a+b-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a+b-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, our deletion formula reads as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig and inverse <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials of the graphic matroids <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</span></p>
