<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=a+b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=a+b-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the deletion formulas for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> look the same, we indicate the proof only for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, as the other follows analogously. Let us call <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> the edge along which the cycles of size <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are glued to form <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the set <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the full ground set <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, the two ground sets of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a}=\mathsf{U}_{a-1,a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a}=\mathsf{U}_{a-1,a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{b}=\mathsf{U}_{b-1,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{b}=\mathsf{U}_{b-1,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, and several other flats for which <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{F}/i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{F}/i)</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes due to the presence of coloops.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a,b}/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a,b}/i</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the disconnected matroid <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{a-2,a-1}\oplus\mathsf{U}_{b-2,b-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{a-2,a-1}\oplus\mathsf{U}_{b-2,b-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptau(\mathsf{C}_{a,b}/i)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptau(\mathsf{C}_{a,b}/i)=0</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Lemma 2.7]</cite>.
As noted in the discussion prior to the statement, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{C}_{a,b}\smallsetminus i=\mathsf{C}_{a+b-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{C}_{a,b}\smallsetminus i=\mathsf{C}_{a+b-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{C}_{a,b}\smallsetminus i}(x)=Q_{\mathsf{U}_{a+b-3,a+b-2}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{C}_{a,b}\smallsetminus i}(x)=Q_{\mathsf{U}_{a+b-3,a+b-2}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. By putting the pieces together and using that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are multiplicative under direct sums (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Lemma 3.1]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Proposition 2.3]</cite>), we obtain the formula of the statement.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
