<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Single element deletions of projective geometries</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we employ Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 ([]) ‣ 2.2. A recapitulation on the Kazhdan–Lusztig invariants ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomialsof matroids under deletion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> in order to compute the inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of a projective geometry with a single element deleted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=V(r,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=V(r,q)</annotation></semantics></math>
be the r-dimensional vector space over the finite field <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote the projective geometry <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(V)</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> over the finite field <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a modular matroid, and so its Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial is the constant <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Proposition 2.14]</cite>). An unpublished result of Vecchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Theorem 3.5]</cite> guarantees that the inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant polynomial, and in fact equals <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q))=q^{\binom{r}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q))=q^{\binom{r}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the projective geometry <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> over the finite field <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}\smallsetminus i}(x)=q^{\binom{r}{2}}-q^{\binom{r-1}{2}}+\left(\frac{q^{r-1}-1}{q-1}q^{\binom{r-2}{2}}-q^{\binom{r-1}{2}}\right)\cdot x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}\smallsetminus i}(x)=q^{\binom{r}{2}}-q^{\binom{r-1}{2}}+\left(\frac{q^{r-1}-1}{q-1}q^{\binom{r-2}{2}}-q^{\binom{r-1}{2}}\right)\cdot x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We start from the formula for <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4 ‣ 1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomialsof matroids under deletion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and substitute <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The lattice of flats of a projective geometry is modular.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The sum in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4 ‣ 1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomialsof matroids under deletion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> is over the set
<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}_{i}=\{G\in\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M}):i\in G\text{ and }G\smallsetminus i\notin\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}_{i}=\{G\in\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M}):i\in G\text{ and }G\smallsetminus i\notin\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the projective geometry <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, a flat <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear subspace. If <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}(G)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}(G)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, removing the point <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> results in a set <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\smallsetminus i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\smallsetminus i</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a subspace, and thus not a flat. If <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}(G)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}(G)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\{i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\{i\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\smallsetminus i=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\smallsetminus i=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a flat. Thus, for <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}=\operatorname{PG}(r-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of all flats containing <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or greater.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the term in the sum is <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)\cdot x^{\rk(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)/2}\cdot Q_{\mathsf{M}/G}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)\cdot x^{\rk(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)/2}\cdot Q_{\mathsf{M}/G}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The matroid <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i</annotation></semantics></math> has lattice of flats isomorphic to the interval <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[i,G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[i,G]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathsf{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{PG}(k-2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{PG}(k-2,q)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\rk(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\rk(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The rank of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> and so <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-zero only if the rank <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1=1</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the sum is over the set of rank 2 flats containing the point <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of such flats is the number of 2-dimensional subspaces of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(r,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(r,q)</annotation></semantics></math> containing a given 1-dimensional subspace, which is equal to the number of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces in the quotient space <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(r,q)/\langle i\rangle\cong V(r-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(r,q)/\langle i\rangle\cong V(r-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. This number is <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}r-1\\
1\end{bmatrix}_{q}=\frac{q^{r-1}-1}{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}r-1\\
1\end{bmatrix}_{q}=\frac{q^{r-1}-1}{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of lines through the point <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each such rank 2 flat <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)=\uptau(\operatorname{PG}(0,q))=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\uptau</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptau(\mathsf{M}|_{G}/i)=\uptau(\operatorname{PG}(0,q))=1</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{PG}(0,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{PG}(0,q)</annotation></semantics></math> has rank 1.</p>
