<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The edges on the left are labelled by <math id="S5.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the edges on the right by <math id="S5.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{3,4,7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{3,4,7\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the bottom one by <math id="S5.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that rank <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matroids without loops are trivially paving, and therefore, corank <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matroids without coloops are copaving. This fact is relevant because the class of elementary split matroids considered by Ferroni and Schröter in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> contains all copaving matroids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, since Ardila and Sanchez <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 8.4]</cite> showed that the inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial is a valuative invariant, we may apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> to compute <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for any corank <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matroid without coloops. In order to state our next result, let us recall that in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> a subset <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stressed</em> if both <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}/A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}/A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}|_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}|_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> are uniform matroids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As the following result shows (once combined with Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 4.2 ‣ 4.2. Glued cycles ‣ 4. Some special cases and examples ‣ Inverse Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomialsof matroids under deletion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), our formulas ultimately only depend on the values of <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝖬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\mathsf{M}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for uniform matroids of corank <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a corank <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matroid without coloops. For each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uplambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\uplambda</mtext><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uplambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of stressed subsets of rank <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and size <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
